  <title>5.8.1: Meaning of a Function Presented Graphically</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01385</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.8.1: Meaning of a Function Presented Graphically</md:title>
    <md:uuid>db3e75be-1675-4188-81ff-7a0dd24483f6</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para>Here is a graph of the accumulated rainfall in Las Vegas, Nevada, in the first 60 days of 2017.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="CONTINUOUS GRAPH WITH TITLE OF ACCUMULATED RAINFALL, LAS VEGAS, NEVADA, X-AXIS LABEL OF DAY OF THE YEAR 2017, AND Y-AXIS LABEL OF RAINFALL IN INCHES. THE X-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 60 IN INCREMENTS OF 10. THE Y-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 1-POINT-6 IN INCREMENTS OF 0-POINT-2.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_10_02_01.png"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para>Use the graph to support your answers to the following questions.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Is the accumulated amount of rainfall a function of time? </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Yes. For each number of days \(d\) between 0 and 60, a certain amount of rain fell up to that time, so there is one particular value for the accumulated rain at any point in time.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Is time a function of accumulated rainfall? </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No. There was no rainfall in the first 10 days. This means that for the amount of rainfall 0 inches, or for the input value of 0, there are lots of times \(d\) that could be the output.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
